<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cambridge</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cambridge is a city in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> on the River Cam, home to the University of Cambridge, the second-oldest university in the English-speaking world, founded around 1209 by scholars leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>. Its colleges trained clergy, officials and scientists, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-newton">Isaac Newton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-darwin">Charles Darwin</a>, and its laboratories saw major discoveries such as the structure of DNA in 1953.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cambridge.jpg" alt="Cambridge" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Christophe BENOIST · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cambridge_-_Kings_College_vue_des_backs.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Cambridge is the county town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridgeshire">Cambridgeshire</a>, on the River Cam about 55 miles north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. It stands on level, low-lying ground just south of the Fens, between 6 and 24 metres above sea level, and the town was historically surrounded by wetlands that were drained as it expanded. Its river link to the farmland around it and its road connections to London made it an important regional trading post.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest clear evidence of settlement is a 3,500-year-old farmstead found at the site of Fitzwilliam College, and an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> settlement stood on Castle Hill from the 1st century BCE. A small Roman fort, Duroliponte, was built on Castle Hill around 70 CE and converted to civilian use some fifty years later. After the Roman withdrawal around 410 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxons</a> were settling the area by the end of the century, and their settlement was called Grantebrycge, Granta bridge. By the 7th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> described it as a little ruined city holding the burial place of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelthryth">Æthelthryth</a>. It sat on the border between the East and Middle Anglian kingdoms.</p><p>Henry I issued the earliest known municipal charter in the early 12th century and granted the town a monopoly on river trade. A charter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> in 1207 permitted the appointment of a mayor, and the first recorded mayor, Harvey FitzEustace, served in 1213. Fairs such as Stourbridge Fair and Midsummer Fair drew merchants from across the country. Hobson's Conduit, built in the early 1600s after repeated outbreaks of pestilence, brought fresh water from Nine Wells into the centre. The railway reached Cambridge in 1845, and with changing patterns of retail distribution it reduced the town's importance as a market town.</p><p>The town expanded rapidly in the 19th century, and inclosure acts of 1801 and 1807 allowed it to spread over the surrounding open fields. Members of the university drew up the first rules of association football in 1848, the Cambridge Rules, first played on Parker's Piece, and they influenced the Football Association rules of 1863.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Cambridge received city status in 1951. Its university, founded in 1209, has buildings that include King's College Chapel, the Cavendish Laboratory and the University Library, one of the largest legal deposit libraries in the world. The city lies at the centre of a high-technology district known as Silicon Fen and is completely enclosed by green belt.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cambridge” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The universities of Oxford and <b>Cambridge</b>, which emerged in the late twelfth and early thirteenth centuries, trained clergy and officials.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>British science produced penicillin, the jet engine, the structure of DNA, discovered at <b>Cambridge</b> in 1953, and the first cloned mammal, Dolly the sheep.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom">United Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…a classic case for scholars of religion of how a new religion emerges from an older one: the <b>Cambridge</b> orientalist Edward Granville Browne, who visited Bahá’u’lláh at Bahjí in 1890 and left the only description of him by a Western visitor, devoted years to documenting the Bábí and…</blockquote><a href="/religion/bahai-faith">Bahá'í Faith · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1943 a terracotta head, which had stood for a year as a scarecrow in a yam field, came to the attention of Bernard Fagg, a colonial administrative officer who had studied archaeology at <b>Cambridge</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/nok-culture">Nok culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Jawaharlal Nehru (1889–1964), educated at Harrow and <b>Cambridge</b>, became the voice of its socialist and internationalist wing and Gandhi’s chosen heir.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · People</a></li><li><blockquote>In Zurich Zwingli’s work was carried on after his death in battle at Kappel in 1531 by Heinrich Bullinger, and in Strasbourg Martin Bucer worked to reconcile the Lutheran and Swiss reformers before ending his life in exile in <b>Cambridge</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · People</a></li></ul></section>
